icular night  it looked like this you could see that  storm and you can see yes you can see  the storm that's because that storm has  a what's called a quite high albedo  feature it's actually contrasted quite  well against the planet does it look  that colourful when you look at it with  your eyes and then there's that that is  interesting most the time it looks like  a yellowy orange color on some nights  particularly around the time of  opposition that's when it's opposite the  Sun and to us and we're in the middle  you'll get the Rings being really really  bright and they'll sort of that's called  the Selig effect Jupiter on the other  hand produces color you can see red and  you can see festoons of blue and all  that sort of thing have you got a  picture  I do yes so you can see here there's  some color here and that the festoons in  that in the central area which is called  the equatorial belt you can see a moon  here that has some detail on it and of  course the Great Red Spot which has been  around for three hundred years most  people would know about that Jupiter is  more colorful than setting to look at ya  visually it is yeah you need a good  night to get good detail but you can see  more color in it the blue festoons in  particular of obvious to the eye in this  day and age there are some amazing  pictures of Saturn going around there's  a space probe currently at Saturn what's  the role for someone like you what's the  point of taking pictures of Saturn on  earth when we can just go on to the NASA  website and look at amazing pictures  Saturn what what why do you do it well  interestingly amateurs can contribute  quite a lot we have the capacity to take  lots and lots of images because there's  thousands of us doing this now  immediately when in 2004 when I first  started doing planetary imaging there  weren't many people around they were  probably 100 or so and contributing now  there's thousands and thousands of  people which is great because at any o